From e2298a919fc3ab7d05ea24cd75df311a5c3cd86c Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: well0nez <8986239+well0nez@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 7 Oct 2026 01:26:40 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 29/32] allwinner: h713: the key-sink service takes no address
 and serves once

Until now the normal world chose what EL3 loaded as this device's HDCP 1.4
identity: PUSH_FROM took a source address, checked only its alignment and
that it lay in the Trusted OS window, and ran the engine task from there -
at any time, as often as asked, also from a running OS.

The release service now has no address parameter and one sink write per
boot:

  - HDCP14_STAGE (0x8200001d) answers where the boot loader leaves the
    stored item: a stage at 0x48a00000, the address the push was measured
    with, fixed in BL31 and asserted at build time to lie in the window.
    It holds two secure-storage slots, the item's original and its copy,
    unchanged as they sit on the eMMC.
  - HDCP14_ONCE (0x8200001e) is the one sink write of this boot: x1 = 0 the
    staged key set, 1 zeros (the wipe the HDCP measurement rule needs), 2
    nothing. Whatever it is and however it ends, the service is closed
    afterwards; later calls, and STAGE, get E_CLOSED (0x17). U-Boot closes
    it with "nothing" when it writes nothing - in its HDCP step, and again
    before it starts an OS with bootm.
  - Before anything reaches the engine, EL3 makes the structure checks that
    so far only U-Boot made (TODO 6.2): magic, the item name in both headers,
    NUL-terminated; an outer length that fits the slot; an inner length equal
    to the outer one minus the inner header and at least 288; a key set that
    is not all 0x00 or 0xff; and both copies agreeing on the 288 bytes. A
    failure names the check on the console and answers E_FORMAT (0x18).
  - The validated 288 bytes are moved to the start of the stage, so the
    engine task is the measured one byte for byte (source 0x48a00000, sink
    0x03041400, key-select 3, decrypt, 288). The whole stage is wiped after.

How far the lock reaches, plainly: it holds against an OS that cannot write
BL31's memory. On this board BL31 sits in DRAM that nothing protects from the
normal world (no TZASC is set up, and a no-map reservation does not stop
/dev/mem), so whoever can write hdcp14_closed can open the service again -
a hurdle, not a boundary. The comment at the top of the file says so.

The window gets its names, SUNXI_TEE_WINDOW_BASE/LIMIT in sunxi_mmap.h,
with where the two addresses come from; the workbench calls use them too.
PUSH_FROM stays for the workbench only (H713_HDCP_RESEARCH=1). The version
call answers (2, 0). The release-gate string "key set from" stays in the
NOTICE line.

Release build: no warnings, bl31.bin 49276 bytes as before (.text stays
within its page). Changed functions: h713_sip_handler (hdcp14_once inlined)
and the new stage_key_set; the linker adds one Cortex-A53 843419 veneer.
The structure checks were run on the host against U-Boot's own parser over
18 synthetic records (no key material): both accept and refuse the same
ones.

Rebased on 09.10.2026 onto 175c6948f (the standby commits) without a
conflict. Measured on the HY310 over FEL (09.10.2026, doku/180), U-Boot and BL31 of this series,
Linux from the eMMC: zeros, then the key set, from the stage
in two boots in a row (Desired, then Enabled); a second push in one boot refused.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
